          text: 'A photon of wavelength $\\lambda$ carries $E = hc/\\lambda$; per mole of photons at 400 nm (violet light) that is $\\dfrac{(6.626\\times10^{-34})(2.998\\times10^{8})(6.022\\times10^{23})}{400\\times10^{-9}} \\approx 299$ kJ/mol, more than the Cl–Cl BDE of 243 kJ/mol. One absorbed photon cleaves one $\\ce{Cl2}$. Polar heterolysis of $\\ce{Cl2}$ in the gas phase would cost over 1000 kJ/mol, because it must separate a positive ion from a negative one with no solvent to help.',
